item message:2 message final at=3127 text="The lamp had been dark for three days, and Maren still climbed the stairs each evening.\n\nOne hundred and twelve steps, worn into shallow bowls at the center by a century of boots. She knew every one by its particular complaint—the seventh creaked, the forty-third had a chip that caught her heel, the ninety-eighth was slick where the salt had gotten under the paint. She climbed them with a bucket of kerosene she no longer needed, because the mechanism had seized in the storm and the parts ship wouldn't come until spring.\n\nAt the top, the great lens sat silent, its brass ribs furred with dust. She wiped them anyway.\n\nThe mainland had stopped asking. The last supply boat had brought tinned meat, a bag of mail she didn't open, and a young man from the service who'd stood in her kitchen with a clipboard and said, gently, that she could come back with him. He'd gestured at the barren rock, the gray sea, the gulls screaming their ugly arguments. No one would blame her, he said.\n\nShe'd poured him tea and told him the fog was coming.\n\nIt came that night, as she'd known it would—a wall of it, soft and total, swallowing the water line by line until the world was only the tower and the sound of something breathing against the glass. And somewhere out in that breathing, she knew, there were boats. Fishing boats with men who'd lost their bearings. A trawler running late. Maybe only a pleasure yacht full of people who'd never thought about the sea at all until it erased everything around them.\n\nSo she did what she had always done. She lit the oil lantern—not the great lamp, just the small one, the emergency light—and she carried it up and set it in the western window, and she stood beside it.\n\nIt was a poor light. A candle against a cathedral. But it was a fixed point. It said: here is land. Here is a thing that does not move.\n\nShe stood until her legs ached. She stood while the fog pressed close and the lantern guttered and the gulls went quiet. She thought about her father, who had kept this light before her, and his mother before him, and how none of them had ever been heroes—only stubborn, only present, only unwilling to let the dark have the last word.\n\nNear dawn, the fog thinned. A shape slid past, close enough that she saw the running lights, red and green, a trawler feeling its way home. On its deck a man looked up. He couldn't have seen her—couldn't have seen the small flame in the high window—but he raised his hand anyway, a wave into the gray, as if he knew.\n\nMaren raised hers back.\n\nThen she went down the hundred and twelve steps, made tea, and waited for evening, when she would climb them again.\n\nThe parts ship came in spring. By then she'd fixed the mechanism herself, with a hairpin and a great deal of swearing, and the great lamp turned once more, sweeping its long white arm across the water.\n\nShe never did open the mail.steered"
